WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.089
Hi there.

00:00:01.089 --> 00:00:05.500
Just before this video starts, I wanted to
briefly explain what it is.

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:10.370
About a week ago, we launched our first new
channel in two years: Extremities.

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:15.580
It’s about why and how the world’s most
remote settlements exist, told with stunning

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:19.190
visuals and exclusive interviews from these
places.

00:00:19.190 --> 00:00:24.050
What you’re about to watch is the first
episode: it’s about Little Bay Islands--a

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:30.510
town that, for complex reasons, ceased to
exist after December 31st, 2019.

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:34.210
At the end of this I’m not going to ask
you to go sign up for CuriosityStream or to

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:38.850
check out HelloFresh or anything: I’m just
going to ask you, if you enjoyed this, to

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:43.960
go subscribe to the Extremities channel, and
check out the two other videos there, because

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:49.200
this is the only one that will ever go up
on Wendover Productions.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.309
With that, please enjoy the video.&nbsp;

00:00:52.309 --> 00:01:01.579
At 5 P.M. on December 31st, 2019, the MV Hazel
McIsaac pulled away from Little Bay Islands.

00:01:01.579 --> 00:01:08.810
On board were the last few holdouts of those
who had called the tiny island hamlet home.

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:13.160
This would be the final time the town’s
regular ferry service pushed off from the

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:19.479
rustic collection of snow-dusted crimson,
off-white, and beige saltbox homes.

00:01:19.479 --> 00:01:24.119
As it exited the protected harbor, the permanent
population of Little Bay Islands plummeted

00:01:24.119 --> 00:01:26.990
from 54 to just two.

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:32.999
For those onboard, and the two still standing
on shore, the weight of the moment hung heavy

00:01:32.999 --> 00:01:34.219
in the air.

00:01:34.219 --> 00:01:39.119
Mike Parsons, who, along with his wife Georgina,
decided to stay, said of the final days that

00:01:39.119 --> 00:01:43.409
a “palpable sadness” had enveloped the
islands.

00:01:43.409 --> 00:01:47.159
Although an all-but-inevitable conclusion,
leaving hurt.

00:01:47.159 --> 00:01:48.979
“Bartlett: This is nowhere.

00:01:48.979 --> 00:01:52.310
There's nothing else there, you know.

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:56.469
But still, people got the love of the land
where they were born on.

00:01:56.469 --> 00:01:57.469
I mean, you know this.”

00:01:57.469 --> 00:02:02.059
As the ferry cut through the low-hanging fog
and faded away in the evening’s dying light,

00:02:02.059 --> 00:02:07.450
and as the 2010s faded from present to past,
a tiny town on a couple tiny islands closed

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:12.180
a chapter of its story that had begun some
200 years prior.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:17.190
What was once a boomtown-gone-bust was now
all but a ghost town.

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:22.250
That day, electricity that had powered the
H.L. Strong Academy, Aunt Edna’s B&amp;B, the

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:27.849
two churches, and the dozens of heartily built
homes wrapping around the harbor, shut off.

00:02:27.849 --> 00:02:33.409
Unopposed by streetlights and the hum of habitation—night
and its accompanying quiet settled over the

00:02:33.409 --> 00:02:37.969
town of two, and Little Bay Islands became
the eighth remote community in Newfoundland

00:02:37.969 --> 00:02:42.230
and Labrador this century to permanently relocate.

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:45.849
The abandonment of Little Bay Islands, and
the reason this small settlement existed in

00:02:45.849 --> 00:02:51.620
the first place, is enmeshed in the intertwined
history, politics, and economics of the North

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:56.030
Atlantic—all of which, across centuries
of European settlement, have revolved around

00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:58.940
a rather nondescript fish.

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:02.470
&nbsp;
By the 2010s, Little Bay Islands—a name

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:07.459
left plural to acknowledge the community’s
straddling of both Little Bay and Mack Islands—was

00:03:07.459 --> 00:03:08.870
on its last leg.

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:14.629
At its height, more than five hundred called
the small-but-bustling town nestled between

00:03:14.629 --> 00:03:17.790
lush forest and rocky coast home.

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:21.260
What brought people to Little Bay Islands
and the coast of Newfoundland, had little

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:25.870
to do with what was on land, but rather what
swam in the surrounding waters.

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:30.780
“Thornhill Verma: But the reason why settlers
predominantly from Europe came to Newfoundland,

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:37.459
Labrador and populated the outports was because
of the proximity to this lush resource of

00:03:37.459 --> 00:03:39.790
wild fish.

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:42.230
And that continues to be the case…”

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:47.120
Beginning with Paleo-Eskimo peoples arriving
around 1,000 BCE, then followed by the Little

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:51.520
Passage peoples two thousand years later,
indigenous hunter-gatherer societies were

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:55.629
the first to recognize the wealth of Newfoundland’s
teeming coasts.

00:03:55.629 --> 00:03:59.730
Not long after the Little Passage peoples
had arrived, though, these waters became a

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:01.760
bit busier.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:06.450
Northern and Western Europeans were next to
identify the region’s oceanic potential—and

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:11.780
unlike contemporary explorers, they weren’t
there for God… but for cod.

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:17.440
Simply put: “Bartlett: [00:10:43] Cod, when
in my time I grew up, and previous to that,

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:18.630
cod was King.”

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:23.400
Cod’s prominence, its centrality to life
in Little Bay Islands, and Newfoundland more

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:30.060
broadly, spans well beyond Wilfred’s lifetime;
going back not just generations, but centuries.

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:34.560
When Norse sailors landed on Newfoundland
hundreds of years prior to Columbus’s crossing,

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:39.789
they skirted from one cold, barren shore to
the next with little more than the prevalent,

00:04:39.789 --> 00:04:43.869
easy-to-catch, high-in-protein fish to fill
their caloric needs.

00:04:43.869 --> 00:04:47.910
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; For the Europeans that followed,
cod didn’t just represent a calorie-dense

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:52.660
source of sustenance, but a resource with
which they could turn a profit.

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:56.639
Although British and Basque fishing ventures
kept tight lips about their favorite spots,

00:04:56.639 --> 00:05:01.910
it’s likely that enterprising vessels cut
across the Atlantic alongside and even before

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:05.210
Columbus and John Cabot in the 1490s.

00:05:05.210 --> 00:05:09.650
Once Cabot had come across Newfoundland though,
the secret was out, as the explorer exclaimed

00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:14.360
that these faraway waters were so well stocked
that all one had to do was stick a basket

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:17.349
in the ocean and it’d fill with fish.

00:05:17.349 --> 00:05:20.940
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; While European explorers
carried with them a penchant to exaggerate,

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:26.690
Cabot’s description of fishing in the Northwest
Atlantic maps rather accurately onto the disposition

00:05:26.690 --> 00:05:27.910
of the Atlantic Cod.

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:33.690
An omnivorous bottom-feeder, cod are both
perpetually lethargic and ceaselessly hungry,

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:38.729
and as such, gladly take bait and don’t
put up much of a fight once on a line.

00:05:38.729 --> 00:05:42.599
Properly treated, cod keeps, too.

00:05:42.599 --> 00:05:49.660
“Bartlett: And cod, one thing about cod
was all all salt, put on salt back then, so,

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:53.660
I mean, you could keep it for a long, long
time, year or two years or three years if

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:54.660
you had to.

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:56.710
I don't know how long you could if you really
need it.

00:05:56.710 --> 00:06:04.010
But usually it was cut, split, and sold and
then taken back to England and Portugal and

00:06:04.010 --> 00:06:05.099
Spain.”

00:06:05.099 --> 00:06:09.560
Countries that, as Catholic, were eager to
find alternatives to warm-blooded meats during

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:10.560
lent.

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:13.690
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; As the fish gained popularity
across Europe—becoming a staple in markets

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:18.420
in the 1500s, and appearing in the pages of
Cervantes’ Don Quixote, Zola’s The Belly

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:23.620
of Paris, and Dumas’ Le Grand Dictionnaire
de Cuisine—European settlements took root

00:06:23.620 --> 00:06:27.250
along the rocky shores of the continent’s
northeastern coast.

00:06:27.250 --> 00:06:32.770
These hearty, self-reliant, coastal communities,
now known as “outports,” drove the growth

00:06:32.770 --> 00:06:36.040
of Newfoundland from the 1700s onward.

00:06:36.040 --> 00:06:40.970
Built on and around the water, Little Bay
Islands—only accessible by boat, angling

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:45.500
out at the rich waters of Notre Dame Bay and
the Grand Banks beyond but hugging the fragmented

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:50.370
and protective coast of Newfoundland—was
the quintessential outport.&nbsp;

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:55.490
The earliest census of Little Bay Islands
dates to 1825, but some locals like Wilfred

00:06:55.490 --> 00:06:59.550
Bartlett believe that British fishers may
well have established year-round habitation

00:06:59.550 --> 00:07:02.009
on the islands decades prior.

00:07:02.009 --> 00:07:08.180
Whether settled in the 1820s or the 1720s,
Little Bay Islands embodies the era of fish-fueled

00:07:08.180 --> 00:07:09.400
European expansion.

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:13.780
And without an abiding climate or much in
the way of arable land for agriculture like

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:19.310
its southern relatives off of Cape Cod, Little
Bay Islands, from its inception, would remain

00:07:19.310 --> 00:07:23.280
a strictly ocean-oriented economy.&nbsp;

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:28.690
From the nineteenth century to the mid-twentieth,
Little Bay Islands enjoyed a period of blissful

00:07:28.690 --> 00:07:30.000
stasis.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:34.780
By 1950, the town, along with the rest of
Newfoundland, had become a part of Canada,

00:07:34.780 --> 00:07:38.830
had added a few more saltbox homes, a couple
new processing plants, and was sheltering

00:07:38.830 --> 00:07:41.150
more modern boats in its harbor.

00:07:41.150 --> 00:07:47.080
But still, Little Bay Islanders went out each
day to work their corner of the cod industry.

00:07:47.080 --> 00:07:50.490
At day's end, they picked their kids up from
the schoolhouse, and headed over to their

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:52.569
local union meetings.

00:07:52.569 --> 00:07:56.599
When they had a chance, they caught up at
the Poacher’s Lounge for a drink.

00:07:56.599 --> 00:08:00.259
When they made it to the weekend, they headed
to the crowded grocery stores and crammed

00:08:00.259 --> 00:08:02.949
into the town’s two churches.

00:08:02.949 --> 00:08:06.689
When winter turned to spring, they planted
root vegetables in home gardens, awaiting

00:08:06.689 --> 00:08:08.819
the peak summer fishing season.

00:08:08.819 --> 00:08:13.909
And when they tired after a long career, they
handed their work off to their children.

00:08:13.909 --> 00:08:20.439
At mid-century, Little Bay Islands still happily
revolved around the same industry it always

00:08:20.439 --> 00:08:21.439
had.

00:08:21.439 --> 00:08:26.219
But, while change isn’t always continuous,
it is inevitable.

00:08:26.219 --> 00:08:32.180
And soon, intruding engines of change showed
up right offshore.

00:08:32.180 --> 00:08:37.910
It came in the shape of a 280-foot British
Fairtry trawling vessel appearing off the

00:08:37.910 --> 00:08:41.130
coast of Newfoundland in 1951.

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:45.591
Alongside an invading fleet of smaller trawlers
from across Europe, the Soviet Union, and

00:08:45.591 --> 00:08:50.420
Japan, this would change cod fishing forever.

00:08:50.420 --> 00:08:54.140
While outsiders weren’t new, as fishers
from as far as Portugal seasonally visited

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:56.980
Little Bay Islands, the technology was.

00:08:56.980 --> 00:09:04.010
“Bartlett: Then all of a sudden, the draggers
got involved where you could drop a net over

00:09:04.010 --> 00:09:09.370
to start of a boat, or over the side for side
trollers, and go drag the bottom of whatever

00:09:09.370 --> 00:09:13.420
is in front of that net certainly comes up,
there's nothing left.

00:09:13.420 --> 00:09:18.670
And not only where we're taking a lot of cod,
but we're taking a lot of bycatch, too, because,

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:23.080
I mean, if you want a lot of cod and there's
other things in there you just throw it back

00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:24.520
in the ocean dead.”

00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:29.010
In just a decade, traditional methods used
by local fishers for generations—hook and

00:09:29.010 --> 00:09:34.540
line, nets, cod traps—were superseded by
floating cities capable of hauling up tons

00:09:34.540 --> 00:09:38.230
of fish and flash freezing them, all while
still at sea.

00:09:38.230 --> 00:09:43.580
At the same time that massive ships consolidated
the fishing industry, politicians sought to

00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:46.680
consolidate Canada’s coastal population.

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:51.990
In an effort to save on expensive government
provided services—ferries, power, healthcare—the

00:09:51.990 --> 00:09:57.660
Centralization Program of 1954 and the Fisheries
Household Resettlement Program of 1965 moved

00:09:57.660 --> 00:10:00.800
thousands from remote outports to population
centers.&nbsp;

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:06.580
In the 1950s and ‘60s, the waters around
Little Bay Islands had become overcrowded

00:10:06.580 --> 00:10:11.690
and towns not so different from it began feeling
pressure to relocate.

00:10:11.690 --> 00:10:17.470
But not only did Little Bay Islands persist
during this period; it thrived—its docks,

00:10:17.470 --> 00:10:22.460
shops, processing plants, churches, and school
hummed with activity.

00:10:22.460 --> 00:10:26.130
For the moment, there were enough fish to
support both the cities at sea pulling it

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:31.520
in by the ton and the town’s continued small-scale
operations.

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:34.360
That moment, however, proved fleeting.

00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:40.660
With innovation, mechanization, and the ruthless
pursuit of efficiency came disaster.

00:10:40.660 --> 00:10:45.260
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; By the 1970s, annual fish
hauls were tripling the tonnage of those from

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:47.790
three decades prior.

00:10:47.790 --> 00:10:50.090
Cod populations couldn’t keep up.

00:10:50.090 --> 00:10:55.520
With collapse on the horizon, Canadian officials
took action—turning back foreign vessels

00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:57.480
and placing quotas on the fish.

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:01.140
But it was far too little, far too late.

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:06.460
By 1990, in some spots, one could hardly find
the fish at all.

00:11:06.460 --> 00:11:11.040
“Bartlett: I faced up Labrador coast five
hundred miles north from where I lived, and

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:14.890
they had a moratorium up there for two years,
couldn't get a cod to eat.

00:11:14.890 --> 00:11:19.000
But, you know, it is, like I said, nobody
listened.”

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:24.820
In 1992, the Canadian government banned nearly
all cod fishing.

00:11:24.820 --> 00:11:29.930
The moratorium aimed to revive the stock over
the course of a few years.

00:11:29.930 --> 00:11:36.540
Today, nearly 30 years later, the moratorium
is still in place.&nbsp;

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:41.560
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The collapse of the cod industry
put Little Bay Islands in an impossible position.

00:11:41.560 --> 00:11:47.120
The town was, the town is, in many ways, a
picture-perfect fishing community.

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:51.670
“Bartlett: Little Bay Islands is one of
the most beautiful communities around.

00:11:51.670 --> 00:11:58.891
It's, it's a beautiful harbor there, in fact,
there's two inlets there, you can get a small

00:11:58.891 --> 00:12:03.040
boat in one end, and a big boat through the
other… it’s an island off of a harbor--that's

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:04.690
what it is.

00:12:04.690 --> 00:12:07.910
And it's... no matter how bad the weather
gets, it’s still sheltered.

00:12:07.910 --> 00:12:12.360
And I guess that's one of the reasons why
it's not a very big island.

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:16.890
People had to go off the island and get enough
wood to keep themselves going for ship building,

00:12:16.890 --> 00:12:22.850
that kind of thing… because according to
an outsider it was it was a small island.

00:12:22.850 --> 00:12:27.350
But it was located to the fishing grounds
so that was the main thing.”&nbsp;

00:12:27.350 --> 00:12:31.170
Now, though, the most important fish was gone.

00:12:31.170 --> 00:12:35.760
As an industry, fishing didn’t line the
pockets of Little Bay Islanders, it didn’t

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:40.980
create massive financial wealth, but it did
support a unique way of life.

00:12:40.980 --> 00:12:46.170
It nurtured the small-town comradery that
only blooms after decades of waiting together

00:12:46.170 --> 00:12:51.670
in the same lines, singing the same hymns,
and drinking the same draft beers.

00:12:51.670 --> 00:12:56.810
It furthered family bonds as mothers and fathers
taught their trades to daughters and sons.

00:12:56.810 --> 00:13:01.380
As the rest of the world moved toward urban
centers, Little Bay Islands offered an alternative

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:07.320
path for those enchanted by a rural, rugged
lifestyle deeply entangled with the surrounding

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:09.050
landscape.

00:13:09.050 --> 00:13:14.160
Fishing made it all possible, and Little Bay
Islands had stayed loyal.

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:19.640
The town had no backup plan, no alternative
industry to pick up the slack should something

00:13:19.640 --> 00:13:21.150
go awry.

00:13:21.150 --> 00:13:24.470
Here, cod was king.

00:13:24.470 --> 00:13:28.070
And now, cod was dead.

00:13:28.070 --> 00:13:34.000
While fishing stayed a consistent economic
driver for centuries, the actual, day-to-day

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:39.020
practice has proved dynamic; requiring flexibility,
and at times, mobility.

00:13:39.020 --> 00:13:44.890
“Thornhill Verma: [00:31:15] So resettlement
is as much a part of outport life in the province

00:13:44.890 --> 00:13:49.700
of Newfoundland and Labrador as the fishing
that brought people there in the first place.”

00:13:49.700 --> 00:13:53.940
Some—the earliest seasonal fishing parties,
and later, families relocating in search of

00:13:53.940 --> 00:13:57.270
better home bases—moved by choice.

00:13:57.270 --> 00:14:02.940
Others—indigenous peoples and outport communities
deemed too far from urban hubs—were pushed

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:05.350
or forced to relocate.

00:14:05.350 --> 00:14:11.200
While Little Bay Islands had dodged resettlement
by choice or force for well over a century,

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:15.910
by the late ‘90s it was becoming difficult
to ignore the creeping thought that their

00:14:15.910 --> 00:14:20.140
time to pull up stakes was quickly arriving.

00:14:20.140 --> 00:14:23.620
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; In the years following the
moratorium, Little Bay Islanders turned their

00:14:23.620 --> 00:14:28.860
efforts to crab, shrimp, and capelin—fish
that have kept Newfoundland’s economy afloat

00:14:28.860 --> 00:14:30.070
through to today.

00:14:30.070 --> 00:14:34.990
“Thornhill Verma: So while the East Coast
cod fishery collapse, what's popularly known

00:14:34.990 --> 00:14:40.570
as the cod moratorium happened in July of
1992 and it's still underway today, I think

00:14:40.570 --> 00:14:46.120
what people don't realize is that this is
still a billion-dollar industry--fisheries.”

00:14:46.120 --> 00:14:47.120
Gone, though, were the jobs that cod provided
on shore and at sea.

00:14:47.120 --> 00:14:48.120
Post-collapse, the newly jobless left in droves.

00:14:48.120 --> 00:14:50.780
“Jenn: [00:30:01] And in the decade that
followed, you had 10 percent of the population

00:14:50.780 --> 00:14:53.070
of Newfoundland and Labrador, move away.”

00:14:53.070 --> 00:14:57.290
As Little Bay Islands’ fishers moved to
the mainland to find work, institutions that

00:14:57.290 --> 00:15:03.780
relied on their presence—the grocery stores,
churches, and school—began to falter.

00:15:03.780 --> 00:15:09.900
In 2009, the crab processing plant, the town's
last major employer, shuttered, leaving the

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:15.810
women who worked the plant little choice but
to relocate or rely on unemployment programs.

00:15:15.810 --> 00:15:20.830
In just a few decades, the bustling town—with
boats whizzing past its wharves by day and

00:15:20.830 --> 00:15:25.960
the Poacher’s Lounge filling to standing
room only by night—had taken on a melancholy

00:15:25.960 --> 00:15:27.300
quiet.

00:15:27.300 --> 00:15:33.630
As families peeled out of town one by one,
the murmurs of wholescale resettlement gained

00:15:33.630 --> 00:15:35.070
momentum.

00:15:35.070 --> 00:15:39.830
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; In Newfoundland and Labrador,
the term resettlement is a loaded one.

00:15:39.830 --> 00:15:44.000
“Bartlett: [00:25:13] I don't know, resettlement
is a bad word for me, I mean, we did that

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:49.200
back in Joey Smallwood’s day and it is a
terrible thing to do, uprooted people that,

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:54.840
you know, that was there all their lives,
and it didn't improve their bit one bit in

00:15:54.840 --> 00:15:55.840
the world.”

00:15:55.840 --> 00:16:00.180
In the wake of the cod collapse, the government
revived the unpopular mid-century policy in

00:16:00.180 --> 00:16:01.830
2010.

00:16:01.830 --> 00:16:06.660
This time around, the resettlement program
offered a proportionally larger payout, prohibited

00:16:06.660 --> 00:16:11.240
the provincial government from initiating
the process, and, if viable, required the

00:16:11.240 --> 00:16:15.420
community to approve the decision by a 90%
vote.

00:16:15.420 --> 00:16:20.740
Still, more amenable terms aside, such a move
was bound to hurt:

00:16:20.740 --> 00:16:25.230
“Thornhill Verma: But I think the reason
why it really strikes an emotional chord in

00:16:25.230 --> 00:16:33.130
Newfoundland and Labrador, is because it feels
like the death of this way of life.

00:16:33.130 --> 00:16:39.910
And so I felt that even when we sold my grandfather's
home in Little Bay East on the southern shores

00:16:39.910 --> 00:16:46.940
of Newfoundland and Labrador, here I had this
physical link to a community that my ancestry

00:16:46.940 --> 00:16:52.700
was tied up in for hundreds of years, and
then for that to be gone and to not be able

00:16:52.700 --> 00:16:58.090
to have that physical link to a place that
I remember growing up and going with my father

00:16:58.090 --> 00:17:03.630
to his childhood home and beachcombing along
the beach and being able to go out on the

00:17:03.630 --> 00:17:11.939
dory with my family members, you know, to
have that access pulled is more than ‘let's

00:17:11.939 --> 00:17:15.000
do something different next year, summer vacation.’

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:20.999
It's this connection to place and culture
and heritage and tradition.

00:17:20.999 --> 00:17:24.179
And so I think that's difficult for people.”

00:17:24.179 --> 00:17:25.879
&nbsp;
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Opportunity created Little

00:17:25.879 --> 00:17:27.350
Bays Islands.

00:17:27.350 --> 00:17:31.679
When opportunity died, the town was bound
to soon follow.

00:17:31.679 --> 00:17:36.590
As the Grand Banks clogged with oversized
trawlers, and fishing transformed from a timeless

00:17:36.590 --> 00:17:42.710
profession to one with an expiration date,
outport youth began to look west for opportunity.

00:17:42.710 --> 00:17:46.799
“Bartlett: And that's what has happened
is no jobs, one time, when the fishery was

00:17:46.799 --> 00:17:50.250
going to go, in the summertime when you're
out of school, well, you got a job at a fish

00:17:50.250 --> 00:17:53.630
plant, working in the fish plant, or you went
in the boat with your father or went down

00:17:53.630 --> 00:17:55.320
to the wharf, cutting out tongues, you made
a living.

00:17:55.320 --> 00:17:56.870
But now there’s nothing for them to do.

00:17:56.870 --> 00:18:00.879
Come out of school there is no… very few
places to pick up a job in Newfoundland and

00:18:00.879 --> 00:18:02.350
Labrador today.”

00:18:02.350 --> 00:18:06.330
In many cases, their own families urged them
to move on.

00:18:06.330 --> 00:18:10.690
“Thornhill Verma: I mean, my grandfather
was the last fisherman in our family, and

00:18:10.690 --> 00:18:11.780
that wasn't by accident.

00:18:11.780 --> 00:18:13.470
That was by design.

00:18:13.470 --> 00:18:19.950
My grandfather and my grandmother wanted to
make sure that their four boys had an education,

00:18:19.950 --> 00:18:25.059
and certainly Newfoundlanders and Labradorians
are all educated, whether that's on the land

00:18:25.059 --> 00:18:30.639
and sea or in a classroom, but they very much
wanted their four boys to receive a classroom

00:18:30.639 --> 00:18:36.390
education and to go on and have careers that
allowed them to leave the ports.”

00:18:36.390 --> 00:18:41.090
As the hope that the fishery would soon recover
faded, Little Bay Islands families and youth

00:18:41.090 --> 00:18:46.750
made their way to the mainland—leaving behind
an aging population deeply tied to the landscape,

00:18:46.750 --> 00:18:51.960
but unmoored from economic opportunities and
far from medical services.

00:18:51.960 --> 00:18:55.460
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; With the processing plants
shuttered, the school empty, the local bar

00:18:55.460 --> 00:19:02.190
dry, the fire department closed, and the town
doctor long gone, Little Bay Islands in 2015

00:19:02.190 --> 00:19:04.630
was a shell of its former self.

00:19:04.630 --> 00:19:10.940
For the 95 aging full-timers, 45-minutes away
from medical facilities and grocery stores,

00:19:10.940 --> 00:19:16.620
assuming fair weather, the time had arrived
to put resettlement up for a vote.

00:19:16.620 --> 00:19:26.250
The result: 85 votes for, 10 against—missing
the required 90% benchmark by 0.53%.

00:19:26.250 --> 00:19:30.220
Four years and zero miracles later, they voted
again.

00:19:30.220 --> 00:19:35.840
This time, it was unanimous: Little Bay Islands
would resettle.

00:19:35.840 --> 00:19:40.470
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; In the months following the
vote, Little Bay Islanders soberly planned

00:19:40.470 --> 00:19:45.659
their next move, packed their belongings,
and, in some cases, converted their homes

00:19:45.659 --> 00:19:49.030
to operate off-grid, anticipating summer visits.

00:19:49.030 --> 00:19:55.019
In addition to the $250,000 Canadian dollars
Little Bay Islanders were to receive for relocating,

00:19:55.019 --> 00:19:57.009
they retained their property.

00:19:57.009 --> 00:20:01.639
Gone though, were the costly government-supplied
amenities.

00:20:01.639 --> 00:20:06.370
No more power, no more snow plowing, and no
more ferry service.

00:20:06.370 --> 00:20:10.799
Those with unwavering bonds to the place where
they grew up, where they raised families,

00:20:10.799 --> 00:20:15.100
where they felt at home, would be back…
but it wouldn’t be the same.

00:20:15.100 --> 00:20:17.619
It couldn’t be.

00:20:17.619 --> 00:20:22.549
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; As the MV Hazel McIsaac disappeared
into the December night, and the Parsons ducked

00:20:22.549 --> 00:20:27.749
back into their newly off-grid home to ring
in the New Year, Little Bay Islands entered

00:20:27.749 --> 00:20:31.940
an era both foreign and familiar.

00:20:31.940 --> 00:20:37.080
Over were the centuries of permanent settlement
and decades of direct connection to mainland

00:20:37.080 --> 00:20:38.080
Canada.

00:20:38.080 --> 00:20:42.710
Returning, though, was the remoteness and
self-sufficiency that defined the outport’s

00:20:42.710 --> 00:20:48.200
earliest days—a uniquely disconnected way
of life that drew many to the islands in the

00:20:48.200 --> 00:20:49.360
first place.&nbsp;

00:20:49.360 --> 00:20:55.259
In recent years, seasonal visitors, sea kayakers,
photographers, authors, and artists have descended

00:20:55.259 --> 00:21:00.090
upon the striking landscapes and quaint coastal
communities of the likes of Little Bay Islands.

00:21:00.090 --> 00:21:04.929
But fishermen, like Wilfred Bartlett, and
grandchildren of fishermen, like Jenn Thornhill

00:21:04.929 --> 00:21:09.559
Verma aren’t yet ready to agree to the Devil’s
bargain that tourism presents.

00:21:09.559 --> 00:21:11.880
“Thornhill Verma: My family still lives
there.

00:21:11.880 --> 00:21:16.129
As much as I'm proud that people go there
to visit, I want people to go and stay for

00:21:16.129 --> 00:21:19.730
a long time, not just a good time.”

00:21:19.730 --> 00:21:23.610
Staying for a long time, though, requires
a healthy fishery.

00:21:23.610 --> 00:21:28.529
A healthy fishery requires something that
Newfoundland outport communities can’t procure

00:21:28.529 --> 00:21:34.299
on their own—future forward, stringent,
protective fishing policy, at the national

00:21:34.299 --> 00:21:35.940
and international level.&nbsp;

00:21:35.940 --> 00:21:41.320
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Outports today, while built
on a foundation of enterprising and independent

00:21:41.320 --> 00:21:44.500
spirit, bend to global forces.

00:21:44.500 --> 00:21:50.850
But then again, Little Bay Islands, and outports
like it, have always been at the mercy of

00:21:50.850 --> 00:21:56.799
economics and politics well beyond their wharves—spanning
the Northern Atlantic and beyond.

00:21:56.799 --> 00:22:01.760
It’s likely too late for Little Bay Islands,
but this doesn’t have to be the end for

00:22:01.760 --> 00:22:03.149
outports.

00:22:03.149 --> 00:22:07.510
The lesson of Little Bay Islands is not that
these places’ rural, small-town lifestyle

00:22:07.510 --> 00:22:08.990
can’t exist.

00:22:08.990 --> 00:22:13.269
Instead, it’s that it can’t exist in a
vacuum.

00:22:13.269 --> 00:22:18.480
While proudly independent and situated well
on the periphery, the survival of these extremities

00:22:18.480 --> 00:22:22.220
hinges on their relationships and ties to
the center.

00:22:22.220 --> 00:22:28.029
And while the people of outports get to decide
when they resettle, it’s much larger forces

00:22:28.029 --> 00:22:31.580
that will decide if they have to.&nbsp;

00:22:31.580 --> 00:22:33.519
I really hope you enjoyed this video.

00:22:33.519 --> 00:22:38.289
As a reminder, this was the pilot episode
of our new channel, Extremities, and this

00:22:38.289 --> 00:22:42.960
is the first and last ever episode of Extremities
that will go up on Wendover.

00:22:42.960 --> 00:22:48.330
So, if you enjoyed this, please go subscribe
to Extremities at the link in the description

00:22:48.330 --> 00:22:51.050
to get all of our future videos.&nbsp;

